<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Android Auto Backup rules for API 31+ (targetSdk is 36, so this is the file that applies;
    backup_rules.xml covers API 28-30).

    This is what makes the ledger survive an uninstall/reinstall without any storage permission.
    AutoBackupManager.performBackup() always writes hisab_auto_backup.json to filesDir/backups/
    before it attempts shared Documents, and restoreIfEmpty() reads that app-private copy first —
    so including this one directory is enough for a reinstall to come back with its data.

    Why an explicit <include> rather than backing everything up:

      * The Room database is deliberately NOT backed up. Room runs in WAL mode, so a live .db is
        only consistent together with its -wal/-shm sidecars; a backup taken mid-write restores a
        torn database. The JSON snapshot has no such hazard, and restoreFromJson() rebuilds the
        database from it.

      * SharedPreferences are deliberately NOT backed up, because "sms_processed_hashes" lives
        there. That file is the INV-1 performance cache, and peek()==true is by definition a safe
        skip. Restoring stale "already seen" marks alongside a JSON-rebuilt database would make
        the catch-up scan skip messages that never made it into the restored ledger — losing
        transactions outright. Room is the sole dedup authority (INV-2); the cache must never be
        restored ahead of it.

    An <include> list is exclusive, so both exclusions hold by construction. If you ever broaden
    this, re-read the two points above first.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="backups/" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="backups/" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
